" y="820" width="26" height="26" rx="7"/><path class="node-icon" transform="translate(283 824) scale(0.75)" d="M5 6.500c0-1.400 3.100-2.500 7-2.500s7 1.100 7 2.500-3.100 2.500-7 2.500-7-1.100-7-2.500z M5 6.500v11c0 1.400 3.100 2.500 7 2.500s7-1.100 7-2.500v-11 M5 12c0 1.400 3.100 2.500 7 2.500s7-1.100 7-2.500"/><text class="node-title" x="319" y="829" font-size="13" text-anchor="start">Agent store</text><text class="node-tech" x="319" y="846" font-size="11" text-anchor="start">Agent definitions + version history</text></g><g class="node" style="--k:var(--c3)"><rect class="node-box" x="529" y="794" width="236" height="78" rx="10"/><rect class="node-icon-bg" x="541" y="820" width="26" height="26" rx="7"/><path class="node-icon" transform="translate(545 824) scale(0.75)" d="M5 6.500c0-1.400 3.100-2.500 7-2.500s7 1.100 7 2.500-3.100 2.500-7 2.500-7-1.100-7-2.500z M5 6.500v11c0 1.400 3.100 2.500 7 2.500s7-1.100 7-2.500v-11 M5 12c0 1.400 3.100 2.500 7 2.500s7-1.100 7-2.500"/><text class="node-title" x="581" y="822" font-size="13" text-anchor="start">Run log</text><text class="node-tech" x="581" y="839" font-size="11" text-anchor="start">Inputs, outputs, ratings, accepted</text><text class="node-tech" x="581" y="853" font-size="11" text-anchor="start">improvements</text></g><g class="node mock" style="--k:var(--muted)"><rect class="node-box" x="791" y="794" width="236" height="78" rx="10"/><rect class="node-icon-bg" x="803" y="820" width="26" height="26" rx="7"/><path class="node-icon" transform="translate(807 824) scale(0.75)" d="M4 20v-12l5-3v15 M9 20v-9h11v9z M12.500 14h1.500 M16 14h1.500 M12.500 17h1.500 M16 17h1.500 M3 20h18"/><text class="node-title" x="843" y="829" font-size="13" text-anchor="start">Company knowledge base</text><text class="node-tech" x="843" y="846" font-size="11" text-anchor="start">SharePoint / intranet (mocked)</text><rect class="tag-state" x="986.2" y="787" width="30.8" height="14" rx="7"/><text class="tag-state-text" x="1001.6" y="797.2" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">MOCK</text></g></svg></div><div class="legend"><span class="key kind" style="--k:var(--muted)"><i></i>People</span><span class="key kind" style="--k:var(--c0)"><i></i>Interface</span><span class="key kind" style="--k:var(--c1)"><i></i>Agent</span><span class="key kind" style="--k:var(--c4)"><i></i>Model</span><span class="key kind" style="--k:var(--c2)"><i></i>Tool or API</span><span class="key kind" style="--k:var(--c3)"><i></i>Data</span><span class="key kind" style="--k:var(--muted)"><i></i>Existing system</span><span class="key ibm"><i>IBM</i>IBM technology</span><span class="key dashed"><i></i>Planned or mocked, not built yet</span></div></figure><p class="fine">Dashed nodes are mocked in the proof of concept with synthetic data and invented policy docs. Interfaces are the real ones — the mock is swapped for the live system in the pilot.</p><div class="split wide"><div class="callout"><h4>IBM technology in this solution</h4><ul><li><b>Agent runner</b> — Executes the agent with grounded instructions on each run</li><li><b>Improvement engine</b> — Turns a rating + comment into a concrete, actionable prompt edit suggestion</li><li><b>Bob IDE</b> — Build environment: agent definitions, tools, tests and the task session report for judges</li></ul></div><div class="decisions"><div class="decision"><h4>Grounding, not fine-tuning</h4><p>Company knowledge is passed as context on each run. No model is fine-tuned, so the PoC runs without data export approval and the knowledge stays current without retraining.</p></div><div class="decision"><h4>Improvement is a suggestion, not an automatic rewrite</h4><p>The agent never updates its own instructions without the user&#x27;s explicit acceptance. This keeps a person in the loop and avoids drift.</p></div><div class="decision"><h4>Mocks behind real interfaces</h4><p>The PoC runs on synthetic data. The policy store, email connector and CRM expose the same API the real systems would, so the mock is replaced by an adapter in the pilot without changing the agent.</p></div><div class="decision"><h4>Built entirely in Bob</h4><p>The task session report in Bob IDE is part of the submission. Building outside Bob loses that evidence for the judges.</p></div></div></div></div></section><section id="impact"><div class="wrap"><header class="sec-head"><div class="kicker"><b>04</b>Impact</div><h2>Each run takes <strong>minutes instead of half an hour</strong>. The agent remembers what good looks like.</h2><p class="lead">The primary metric is time-to-acceptable-output per recurring task. The secondary metric is rating trajectory: do accepted improvements lead to higher ratings on the next run?</p></header><div class="metrics"><div class="metric"><div class="metric-name">Time to acceptable output<span class="basis assumption" title="Before: estimated from 3 runs × 5 min iteration + 25 min total. After: target for the PoC. Pilot will measure."><i></i>Assumption</span></div><div class="bars"><div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-tag">Before</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar before" style="width:100%"></div></div><span class="bar-value">40 <span class="unit">min per run</span></span></div><div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-tag">After</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar after" style="width:15%"></div></div><span class="bar-value"><b>6</b> <span class="unit">min per run</span></span></div></div><div class="delta big good">7× less</div></div><div class="metric"><div class="metric-name">Prompt setup time at session start<span class="basis assumption" title="Before: employee rewrites prompt from memory. After: agent retains instructions between sessions."><i></i>Assumption</span></div><div class="bars"><div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-tag">Before</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar before" style="width:100%"></div></div><span class="bar-value">8 <span class="unit">min</span></span></div><div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-tag">After</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar after" style="width:12.5%"></div></div><span class="bar-value"><b>1</b> <span class="unit">min</span></span></div></div><div class="delta big good">8× less</div></div><div class="metric"><div class="metric-name">Agent rating after 5 runs<span class="basis assumption" title="Target: accepted improvements measurably raise the average rating over 5 runs. Pilot will measure."><i></i>Assumption</span></div><div class="bars"><div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-tag">Before</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar before" style="width:50%"></div></div><span class="bar-value">2 <span class="unit">out of 5</span></span></div><div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-tag">After</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar after" style="width:100%"></div></div><span class="bar-value"><b>4</b> <span class="unit">out of 5</span></span></div></div><div class="delta big good">×2</div></div><div class="metric"><div class="metric-name">Runs before first acceptable output<span class="basis assumption" title="Pilot measurement: compare with and without improvement loop active."><i></i>Assumption</span></div><div class="bars"><div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-tag">Before</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar before" style="width:100%"></div></div><span class="bar-value">3 <span class="unit">runs</span></span></div><div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-tag">After</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar after" style="width:33.3%"></div></div><span class="bar-value"><b>1</b> <span class="unit">runs</span></span></div></div><div class="delta big good">−67%</div></div></div><figure class="card"><figcaption>Indicative yearly value per 100 knowledge workers</figcaption><svg class="diagram waterfall" viewBox="0 0 720 310" role="img" aria-label="Indicative yearly value per 100 knowledge workers" direction="ltr" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line class="axis" x1="8" x2="712" y1="260" y2="260"/><rect class="wf pos" x="36.4" y="110.4" width="84" height="149.6" rx="3"/><line class="wf-link" x1="120.4" x2="177.2" y1="110.4" y2="110.4"/><text class="wf-value pos" x="78.4" y="103.4" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle">+90</text><text class="wf-label" x="78.4" y="278" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">Time returned (prompt</text><text class="wf-label" x="78.4" y="291.8" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">iteration)</text><rect class="wf pos" x="177.2" y="67.2" width="84" height="43.2" rx="3"/><line class="wf-link" x1="261.2" x2="318" y1="67.2" y2="67.2"/><text class="wf-value pos" x="219.2" y="60.2" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle">+26</text><text class="wf-label" x="219.2" y="278" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">Time returned (session</text><text class="wf-label" x="219.2" y="291.8" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">setup)</text><rect class="wf pos" x="318" y="34" width="84" height="33.2" rx="3"/><line class="wf-link" x1="402" x2="458.8" y1="34" y2="34"/><text class="wf-value pos" x="360" y="27" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle">+20</text><text class="wf-label" x="360" y="278" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">Error reduction (wrong</text><text class="wf-label" x="360" y="291.8" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">output forwarded)</text><rect class="wf neg" x="458.8" y="34" width="84" height="29.9" rx="3"/><line class="wf-link" x1="542.8" x2="599.6" y1="63.9" y2="63.9"/><text class="wf-value neg" x="500.8" y="27" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle">−18</text><text class="wf-label" x="500.8" y="278" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">Platform and model run</text><text class="wf-label" x="500.8" y="291.8" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">cost</text><rect class="wf total" x="599.6" y="63.9" width="84" height="196.1" rx="3"/><text class="wf-value total" x="641.6" y="56.9" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle">118</text><text class="wf-label" x="641.6" y="278" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">Net value, year one</text><text class="wf-unit" x="8" y="14" font-size="11">thousand USD per year</text></svg><ul class="fine basis-list"><li>Time returned (prompt iteration): <span class="basis assumption" title="45 min/week × 100 workers × 48 weeks × $40/hr loaded"><i></i>Assumption</span> 45 min/week × 100 workers × 48 weeks × $40/hr loaded</li><li>Time returned (session setup): <span class="basis assumption" title="7 min/day × 100 workers × 240 days × $40/hr loaded"><i></i>Assumption</span> 7 min/day × 100 workers × 240 days × $40/hr loaded</li><li>Error reduction (wrong output forwarded): <span class="basis assumption" title="Estimated 2 rework events/worker/month at 30 min each; halved with rated outputs"><i></i>Assumption</span> Estimated 2 rework events/worker/month at 30 min each; halved with rated outputs</li><li>Platform and model run cost: <span class="basis estimate" title="watsonx.ai API usage at 100 workers × 5 runs/day × avg 2k tokens"><i></i>Estimate</span> watsonx.ai API usage at 100 workers × 5 runs/day × avg 2k tokens</li></ul></figure><div class="callout"><h4>Where it goes next</h4><ul><li>The same platform handles any recurring structured-output task: legal clause drafting, HR onboarding emails, finance commentary, ops incident reports.</li><li>An organisation of 1 000 knowledge workers scales the value waterfall by 10× with no change to the architecture.</li><li>A connector catalog (calendar, CRM, Drive, Slack) turns the output step from text to direct action — each connector is a configuration change, not a code change.</li></ul></div></div></section><section id="alternatives"><div class="wrap"><header class="sec-head"><div class="kicker"><b>05</b>Alternatives</div><h2>Every tool lets you prompt. None of them <strong>remembers what you meant by good</strong>.</h2></header><div class="scroll"><table class="matrix"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Learns from feedback</th><th>Grounds on company docs</th><th>No-code for employees</th><th>Works in minutes</th><th>IBM technology</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=""><th scope="row">Do nothing<small>employees keep iterating manually</small></th><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td></tr><tr class=""><th scope="row">ChatGPT / Copilot<small>general-purpose AI chat</small></th><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td><td><span class="mark partial" title="partly"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td></tr><tr class=""><th scope="row">watsonx Orchestrate<small>IBM&#x27;s no-code agent platform</small></th><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td></tr><tr class=""><th scope="row">Custom agent framework<small>developer-built, LangChain etc.</small></th><td><span class="mark partial" title="partly"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td><td><span class="mark partial" title="partly"></span></td></tr><tr class="ours"><th scope="row">Self-Improving Agent Studio</th><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="legend"><span class="key"><span class="mark yes"></span>yes</span><span class="key"><span class="mark partial"></span>partly</span><span class="key"><span class="mark no"></span>no</span></div><div class="callout"><h4>What is different here</h4><p>Every alternative on the list produces an output. Only this solution closes the loop: when the output is wrong, the agent proposes a change to its own instructions, the user accepts or edits it, and the next run starts better. That loop — feedback to suggestion to acceptance to improved run — is absent from every listed alternative and from most commercial agent builders.</p></div></div></section><section id="demo"><div class="wrap"><header class="sec-head"><div class="kicker"><b>06</b>Demo</div><h2>Four minutes, one complaint, the loop closes on screen</h2><p class="lead">One customer service manager. One complaint. The audience watches the agent get better in real time.</p></header><ol class="strip"><li><div class="strip-num">1</div><h4>Build the agent in 90 seconds<span class="secs">0:00–1:30</span></h4><p>Manager fills three fields: input (complaint text), output (response email + internal action note), context (service policy doc). Clicks Save.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">2</div><h4>First run — acceptable but generic<span class="secs">1:30–2:10</span></h4><p>Complaint CMP-0001 is pasted. The agent returns a polished but overly formal email draft. The audience recognises the problem.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">3</div><h4>Rate and comment<span class="secs">2:10–2:30</span></h4><p>Manager clicks 3 stars, types &#x27;Too formal — more human, use their name&#x27;. Clicks Suggest improvement.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">4</div><h4>The suggestion appears<span class="secs">2:30–3:00</span></h4><p>The studio shows: &#x27;Add instruction: address the customer by name; open with empathy before policy.&#x27; Manager clicks Accept.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">5</div><h4>Second run — visibly better<span class="secs">3:00–3:45</span></h4><p>Same complaint, new run. The draft opens with the customer&#x27;s name and a human acknowledgement. Rating jumps to 5 stars without prompting.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">6</div><h4>The loop is the product<span class="secs">3:45–4:00</span></h4><p>The agent definition now carries the accepted improvement. Every future run starts from this better baseline. The improvement is shown in the run log.</p></li></ol><div class="tiles"><div class="tile"><div class="tile-value">4 min</div><div class="tile-label">total demo runtime</div><span class="basis estimate" title="Scripted and timed with the invented data."><i></i>Estimate</span></div><div class="tile"><div class="tile-value">0</div><div class="tile-label">slides — the demo is the product</div><span class="basis estimate" title="The 3-step UI is self-explanatory."><i></i>Estimate</span></div></div></div></section><section id="adoption"><div class="wrap"><header class="sec-head"><div class="kicker"><b>07</b>Adoption</div><h2>A knowledge worker is productive in the <strong>first five minutes</strong></h2><p class="lead">No IT request. No training course. The agent builder is three fields and a Run button. The first useful output arrives before the employee finishes reading the instructions.</p></header><ol class="strip"><li><div class="strip-num">1</div><h4>Open the studio</h4><p>Link in the company intranet or a pinned message in the team channel. No install, no new login.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">2</div><h4>Describe your task in plain language</h4><p>Three fields: what you will give it, what you want back, which company doc it should follow. Each field has a placeholder example.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">3</div><h4>Run it once</h4><p>Paste your real input — a complaint, a note, a request — and click Run. The first output arrives in seconds.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">4</div><h4>Rate and let it improve</h4><p>If the result is not quite right, give it stars and a comment. Accept the suggestion. Run again. Three iterations are typical before the agent matches your standard.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">5</div><h4>Reuse tomorrow</h4><p>The agent is saved with your name. Tomorrow it starts from where you left it — the improvements are already in.</p></li></ol><div class="tiles"><div class="tile"><div class="tile-value">5 min</div><div class="tile-label">to first useful output</div><span class="basis assumption" title="Target for the PoC. To be timed with two real users in the pilot."><i></i>Assumption</span></div><div class="tile"><div class="tile-value">3 runs</div><div class="tile-label">typical improvement cycles before the agent matches user standard</div><span class="basis assumption" title="To be measured in the pilot from the run log."><i></i>Assumption</span></div></div></div></section><section id="roadmap"><div class="wrap"><header class="sec-head"><div class="kicker"><b>08</b>Roadmap</div><h2>A working loop in three weeks. A real organisation in ten.</h2></header><div class="phases" style="--n:3"><div class="phase"><div class="phase-dot"></div><div class="phase-when">Weeks 1–3</div><h4>Proof of concept</h4><ul><li>One agent type: customer complaint handler</li><li>Synthetic complaints, mocked policy store and CRM</li><li>watsonx.ai inference + improvement suggestion</li><li>3-step UI: Define → Test → Improve</li></ul><div class="gate"><span>Gate</span>Improvement loop closes end-to-end: a rating + comment produces a visible improvement on the next run for at least 8 of 10 planted test cases.</div></div><div class="phase"><div class="phase-dot"></div><div class="phase-when">Weeks 4–10</div><h4>Pilot</h4><ul><li>Two real knowledge workers, one department</li><li>Live policy docs from SharePoint</li><li>Email connector wired (draft only, no auto-send)</li><li>Rating trajectory measured over 20 runs per user</li></ul><div class="gate"><span>Gate</span>Average rating after 5 improvement cycles is at least 1 star higher than the first run, measured on real tasks.</div></div><div class="phase"><div class="phase-dot"></div><div class="phase-when">Quarter 2</div><h4>Production</h4><ul><li>Self-service onboarding for all departments</li><li>Connector catalog (calendar, CRM, Drive)</li><li>Manager review dashboard</li><li>Agent sharing between team members</li></ul><div class="gate"><span>Gate</span>100 active agents across 3 departments; median time-to-acceptable-output under 5 minutes.</div></div></div></div></section><section id="criteria"><div class="wrap"><header class="sec-head"><div class="kicker"><b>09</b>Criteria</div><h2>How this entry answers the four judging criteria</h2></header><div class="grid two"><div class="card crit"><h4>Completeness and feasibility</h4><ul><li>One agent type works end-to-end in the PoC on synthetic data</li><li>Architecture names every IBM component, every mock, and every out-of-scope item</li><li>Three-phase roadmap with a measurable gate after each phase</li><li>Week-1 gate is specific: improvement loop closes on 8 of 10 test cases</li></ul><p class="gap"><span>Open</span>The live policy store connector and the email send connector are designed but not built. The pilot gate is a target, not a measurement.</p></div><div class="card crit"><h4>Creativity and innovation</h4><ul><li>The feedback-to-improvement loop closes without human prompt engineering — the agent proposes its own edit</li><li>No commercial agent builder on the market offers a persistent, user-driven improvement loop on the agent&#x27;s own instructions</li><li>The improvement is a suggestion the user accepts, keeping a person in the loop — not an automatic rewrite</li></ul><p class="gap"><span>Open</span>No formal market search was done. The &#x27;no product does this&#x27; claim is based on the team&#x27;s knowledge of available tools.</p></div><div class="card crit"><h4>Design and usability</h4><ul><li>Three fields to define an agent — no configuration beyond plain language</li><li>The demo takes four minutes with no narration of the UI — it is self-explanatory</li><li>An employee is productive before the first training session ends</li></ul><p class="gap"><span>Open</span>Usability tested only by the team so far. A usability pass with one real user is planned before submission.</p></div><div class="card crit"><h4>Effectiveness and efficiency</h4><ul><li>The problem is frequent (daily, for a large share of knowledge workers)</li><li>The improvement loop creates a measurable baseline: rating trajectory over runs</li><li>The same architecture scales to any recurring structured-output task in any department</li></ul><p class="gap"><span>Open</span>All impact numbers are assumptions. The pilot will replace them with measurements.</p></div></div></div></section><section id="risks"><div class="wrap"><header class="sec-head"><div class="kicker"><b>10</b>Risks</div><h2>What could go wrong, and what we assumed</h2></header><div class="scroll"><table class="plain"><thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Level</th><th>What we do about it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Improvement suggestions are generic and do not reflect the user&#x27;s actual intent</th><td><span class="level high">high</span></td><td>Week-1 gate: generate one demonstrably useful suggestion from a thumbs-down + one comment. If the quality is poor, narrow the suggestion to a set of pre-defined improvement patterns before the demo.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">The agent improvement loop causes drift — accepted improvements degrade quality after several cycles</th><td><span class="level medium">medium</span></td><td>Every accepted improvement is versioned. The user can revert to any previous version. The run log shows the rating trajectory so drift is visible.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Company knowledge base is too unstructured to use as grounding context</th><td><span class="level medium">medium</span></td><td>The PoC uses two hand-written invented policy docs. The pilot starts with a curated set of 5–10 real documents, not the whole intranet.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">The demo depends on watsonx.ai API availability during the presentation</th><td><span class="level low">low</span></td><td>Pre-record the two key runs (first output and improved output) as a fallback video. The live demo runs first; the video is only used if the API is unavailable.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="scroll"><table class="plain"><thead><tr><th>Assumption</th><th>How to check it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">A knowledge worker spends ~45 minutes per week iterating prompts by hand across all recurring tasks</th><td>Ask two workers to time-log their AI tool use for one week before the pilot starts. Count iterations per task type.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3 improvement cycles bring an agent from a first run to a user&#x27;s accepted standard</th><td>Measure from the run log in the pilot: count accepted improvements before the user stops rating below 4 stars.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">10–30% of a white-collar workforce does recurring structured-output work</th><td>Pull role-level headcount from HR; count roles whose primary output is a recurring document type.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">The indicative value waterfall of $118k/year for 100 workers is order-of-magnitude correct</th><td>Replace the $40/hr loaded rate with the organisation&#x27;s actual figure; replace the 45 min/week estimate with the time-log measurement.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout"><h4>Sources</h4><ul><li><b>Invented demo data</b> challenge/data/ — synthetic complaints, service reps and agent runs generated by synth.py with seed 42. Manifest at challenge/data/manifest.json.</li><li><b>Invented policy docs</b> challenge/data/policies/ — service_policy.txt and escalation_guide.txt. Plainly synthetic; no real company procedures.</li><li><b>Judging criteria</b> IBM Bob Challenge judging rubric as documented in the challenge-kit pack.</li></ul></div></div></section><footer><div class="wrap"><span>Team Agent Builder · 2025-07-10</span><span>Numbers carry a mark: measured, estimate or assumption. Built with IBM Bob and the challenge-kit pack.</span></div></footer><script>(function(){if(!document.body.classList.contains('present'))return;
