"row">Do nothing<small>employees keep iterating manually</small></th><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td></tr><tr class=""><th scope="row">ChatGPT / Copilot<small>general-purpose AI chat</small></th><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td><td><span class="mark partial" title="partly"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td></tr><tr class=""><th scope="row">watsonx Orchestrate<small>IBM&#x27;s no-code agent platform</small></th><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td></tr><tr class=""><th scope="row">Custom agent framework<small>developer-built, LangChain etc.</small></th><td><span class="mark partial" title="partly"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td><td><span class="mark no" title="no"></span></td><td><span class="mark partial" title="partly"></span></td></tr><tr class="ours"><th scope="row">Self-Improving Agent Studio</th><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td><td><span class="mark yes" title="yes"></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="legend"><span class="key"><span class="mark yes"></span>yes</span><span class="key"><span class="mark partial"></span>partly</span><span class="key"><span class="mark no"></span>no</span></div><div class="callout"><h4>What is different here</h4><p>Every alternative on the list produces an output. Only this solution closes the loop: when the output is wrong, the agent proposes a change to its own instructions, the user accepts or edits it, and the next run starts better. That loop — feedback to suggestion to acceptance to improved run — is absent from every listed alternative and from most commercial agent builders.</p></div></div></section><section id="demo"><div class="wrap"><header class="sec-head"><div class="kicker"><b>06</b>Demo</div><h2>Four minutes, one complaint, the loop closes on screen</h2><p class="lead">One customer service manager. One complaint. The audience watches the agent get better in real time.</p></header><ol class="strip"><li><div class="strip-num">1</div><h4>Build the agent in 90 seconds<span class="secs">0:00–1:30</span></h4><p>Manager fills three fields: input (complaint text), output (response email + internal action note), context (service policy doc). Clicks Save.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">2</div><h4>First run — acceptable but generic<span class="secs">1:30–2:10</span></h4><p>Complaint CMP-0001 is pasted. The agent returns a polished but overly formal email draft. The audience recognises the problem.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">3</div><h4>Rate and comment<span class="secs">2:10–2:30</span></h4><p>Manager clicks 3 stars, types &#x27;Too formal — more human, use their name&#x27;. Clicks Suggest improvement.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">4</div><h4>The suggestion appears<span class="secs">2:30–3:00</span></h4><p>The studio shows: &#x27;Add instruction: address the customer by name; open with empathy before policy.&#x27; Manager clicks Accept.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">5</div><h4>Second run — visibly better<span class="secs">3:00–3:45</span></h4><p>Same complaint, new run. The draft opens with the customer&#x27;s name and a human acknowledgement. Rating jumps to 5 stars without prompting.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">6</div><h4>The loop is the product<span class="secs">3:45–4:00</span></h4><p>The agent definition now carries the accepted improvement. Every future run starts from this better baseline. The improvement is shown in the run log.</p></li></ol><div class="tiles"><div class="tile"><div class="tile-value">4 min</div><div class="tile-label">total demo runtime</div><span class="basis estimate" title="Scripted and timed with the invented data."><i></i>Estimate</span></div><div class="tile"><div class="tile-value">0</div><div class="tile-label">slides — the demo is the product</div><span class="basis estimate" title="The 3-step UI is self-explanatory."><i></i>Estimate</span></div></div></div></section><section id="adoption"><div class="wrap"><header class="sec-head"><div class="kicker"><b>07</b>Adoption</div><h2>A knowledge worker is productive in the <strong>first five minutes</strong></h2><p class="lead">No IT request. No training course. The agent builder is three fields and a Run button. The first useful output arrives before the employee finishes reading the instructions.</p></header><ol class="strip"><li><div class="strip-num">1</div><h4>Open the studio</h4><p>Link in the company intranet or a pinned message in the team channel. No install, no new login.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">2</div><h4>Describe your task in plain language</h4><p>Three fields: what you will give it, what you want back, which company doc it should follow. Each field has a placeholder example.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">3</div><h4>Run it once</h4><p>Paste your real input — a complaint, a note, a request — and click Run. The first output arrives in seconds.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">4</div><h4>Rate and let it improve</h4><p>If the result is not quite right, give it stars and a comment. Accept the suggestion. Run again. Three iterations are typical before the agent matches your standard.</p></li><li><div class="strip-num">5</div><h4>Reuse tomorrow</h4><p>The agent is saved with your name. Tomorrow it starts from where you left it — the improvements are already in.</p></li></ol><div class="tiles"><div class="tile"><div class="tile-value">5 min</div><div class="tile-label">to first useful output</div><span class="basis assumption" title="Target for the PoC. To be timed with two real users in the pilot."><i></i>Assumption</span></div><div class="tile"><div class="tile-value">3 runs</div><div class="tile-label">typical improvement cycles before the agent matches user standard</div><span class="basis assumption" title="To be measured in the pilot from the run log."><i></i>Assumption</span></div></div></div></section><section id="roadmap"><div class="wrap"><header class="sec-head"><div class="kicker"><b>08</b>Roadmap</div><h2>A working loop in three weeks. A real organisation in ten.</h2></header><div class="phases" style="--n:3"><div class="phase"><div class="phase-dot"></div><div class="phase-when">Weeks 1–3</div><h4>Proof of concept</h4><ul><li>One agent type: customer complaint handler</li><li>Synthetic complaints, mocked policy store and CRM</li><li>watsonx.ai inference + improvement suggestion</li><li>3-step UI: Define → Test → Improve</li></ul><div class="gate"><span>Gate</span>Improvement loop closes end-to-end: a rating + comment produces a visible improvement on the next run for at least 8 of 10 planted test cases.</div></div><div class="phase"><div class="phase-dot"></div><div class="phase-when">Weeks 4–10</div><h4>Pilot</h4><ul><li>Two real knowledge workers, one department</li><li>Live policy docs from SharePoint</li><li>Email connector wired (draft only, no auto-send)</li><li>Rating trajectory measured over 20 runs per user</li></ul><div class="gate"><span>Gate</span>Average rating after 5 improvement cycles is at least 1 star higher than the first run, measured on real tasks.</div></div><div class="phase"><div class="phase-dot"></div><div class="phase-when">Quarter 2</div><h4>Production</h4><ul><li>Self-service onboarding for all departments</li><li>Connector catalog (calendar, CRM, Drive)</li><li>Manager review dashboard</li><li>Agent sharing between team members</li></ul><div class="gate"><span>Gate</span>100 active agents across 3 departments; median time-to-acceptable-output under 5 minutes.</div></div></div></div></section><section id="criteria"><div class="wrap"><header class="sec-head"><div class="kicker"><b>09</b>Criteria</div><h2>How this entry answers the four judging criteria</h2></header><div class="grid two"><div class="card crit"><h4>Completeness and feasibility</h4><ul><li>One agent type works end-to-end in the PoC on synthetic data</li><li>Architecture names every IBM component, every mock, and every out-of-scope item</li><li>Three-phase roadmap with a measurable gate after each phase</li><li>Week-1 gate is specific: improvement loop closes on 8 of 10 test cases</li></ul><p class="gap"><span>Open</span>The live policy store connector and the email send connector are designed but not built. The pilot gate is a target, not a measurement.</p></div><div class="card crit"><h4>Creativity and innovation</h4><ul><li>The feedback-to-improvement loop closes without human prompt engineering — the agent proposes its own edit</li><li>No commercial agent builder on the market offers a persistent, user-driven improvement loop on the agent&#x27;s own instructions</li><li>The improvement is a suggestion the user accepts, keeping a person in the loop — not an automatic rewrite</li></ul><p class="gap"><span>Open</span>No formal market search was done. The &#x27;no product does this&#x27; claim is based on the team&#x27;s knowledge of available tools.</p></div><div class="card crit"><h4>Design and usability</h4><ul><li>Three fields to define an agent — no configuration beyond plain language</li><li>The demo takes four minutes with no narration of the UI — it is self-explanatory</li><li>An employee is productive before the first training session ends</li></ul><p class="gap"><span>Open</span>Usability tested only by the team so far. A usability pass with one real user is planned before submission.</p></div><div class="card crit"><h4>Effectiveness and efficiency</h4><ul><li>The problem is frequent (daily, for a large share of knowledge workers)</li><li>The improvement loop creates a measurable baseline: rating trajectory over runs</li><li>The same architecture scales to any recurring structured-output task in any department</li></ul><p class="gap"><span>Open</span>All impact numbers are assumptions. The pilot will replace them with measurements.</p></div></div></div></section><section id="risks"><div class="wrap"><header class="sec-head"><div class="kicker"><b>10</b>Risks</div><h2>What could go wrong, and what we assumed</h2></header><div class="scroll"><table class="plain"><thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Level</th><th>What we do about it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Improvement suggestions are generic and do not reflect the user&#x27;s actual intent</th><td><span class="level high">high</span></td><td>Week-1 gate: generate one demonstrably useful suggestion from a thumbs-down + one comment. If the quality is poor, narrow the suggestion to a set of pre-defined improvement patterns before the demo.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">The agent improvement loop causes drift — accepted improvements degrade quality after several cycles</th><td><span class="level medium">medium</span></td><td>Every accepted improvement is versioned. The user can revert to any previous version. The run log shows the rating trajectory so drift is visible.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Company knowledge base is too unstructured to use as grounding context</th><td><span class="level medium">medium</span></td><td>The PoC uses two hand-written invented policy docs. The pilot starts with a curated set of 5–10 real documents, not the whole intranet.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">The demo depends on watsonx.ai API availability during the presentation</th><td><span class="level low">low</span></td><td>Pre-record the two key runs (first output and improved output) as a fallback video. The live demo runs first; the video is only used if the API is unavailable.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="scroll"><table class="plain"><thead><tr><th>Assumption</th><th>How to check it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">A knowledge worker spends ~45 minutes per week iterating prompts by hand across all recurring tasks</th><td>Ask two workers to time-log their AI tool use for one week before the pilot starts. Count iterations per task type.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3 improvement cycles bring an agent from a first run to a user&#x27;s accepted standard</th><td>Measure from the run log in the pilot: count accepted improvements before the user stops rating below 4 stars.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">10–30% of a white-collar workforce does recurring structured-output work</th><td>Pull role-level headcount from HR; count roles whose primary output is a recurring document type.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">The indicative value waterfall of $118k/year for 100 workers is order-of-magnitude correct</th><td>Replace the $40/hr loaded rate with the organisation&#x27;s actual figure; replace the 45 min/week estimate with the time-log measurement.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout"><h4>Sources</h4><ul><li><b>Invented demo data</b> challenge/data/ — synthetic complaints, service reps and agent runs generated by synth.py with seed 42. Manifest at challenge/data/manifest.json.</li><li><b>Invented policy docs</b> challenge/data/policies/ — service_policy.txt and escalation_guide.txt. Plainly synthetic; no real company procedures.</li><li><b>Judging criteria</b> IBM Bob Challenge judging rubric as documented in the challenge-kit pack.</li></ul></div></div></section><footer><div class="wrap"><span>Team Agent Builder · 2025-07-10</span><span>Numbers carry a mark: measured, estimate or assumption. Built with IBM Bob and the challenge-kit pack.</span></div></footer><s